  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe rest of today through Thursday...\n\nFog will impact portions of the region again tonight into Saturday\nmorning. The highest chance (60-80%) will be in low-lying areas,\nespecially along river channels. Fog could become locally dense, \nsignificantly reducing visibility in spots.\n\nBy this evening, isolated to scattered (20-50% chance) showers\nwill develop over the Absaroka/Beartooth mountains as weak\nshortwaves enter the region. Then, these showers will spread into\nthe plains Saturday morning into afternoon as a slightly stronger\nshortwave moves through the region. Overall, precipitation totals\nwill remain light with this system, with only a 10-30% chance of\nmore than 0.10\" outside of the mountains. The mountains may see an\ninch of snow (50% chance) above 8,500 ft, with locally higher \namounts over the highest south facing slopes.\n\nSunday into Monday, a stronger weather system arrives, bringing a\nbetter chance (70-90%) for widespread precipitation. Precip begins\nover the Absaroka/Beartooth mountains Sunday morning, spreading\neast through the afternoon and evening, and exiting Monday\nafternoon into evening. Models continue to trend toward favoring \nsoutheast Montana and north central and northeast Wyoming for \nprecip, with a 50-70% chance of more than 0.50\" of rain and a \n20-40% chance for more than 1\". Snow remains mostly limited to the\nmountains as snow levels will begin above 9,000 ft Sunday \nafternoon, falling to around 6,500 ft by Monday afternoon (when \nthe system begins to exit the region). As a result, precip may \nbegin as rain or a rain/snow mix over the mountains before \ntransitioning to snow. Currently, there is a 45% chance for more\nthan 6 inches of snow over the northern Bighorns and a 40% chance\nof the same over the Absaroka/Beartooth mountains. As the system\nexits Monday evening, there is a low chance (<20%) for a few\nsnowflakes over the higher hills.\n\nLate Sunday into early Monday, a cold front moves through the\nregion. The primary impact will be widespread breezy winds Monday\nof 20 to 40 mph. Far southeast Montana along the Dakota border and\nthe Bighorn foothills are expected to see the strongest winds,\nwith a 60% chance of gusts stronger than 50 mph and a 20% chance\nof gusts stronger than 60 mph. Winds are expected to peak during \nthe day Monday before coming to an end Monday night.\n\nTuesday into Thursday look to be mostly warm and dry under upper\nlevel ridging. The current track of a trough moving through south\nof the region will help to keep temperatures near seasonable (low\n50s) and may allow an occasional isolated shower (<20% chance),\nmainly over the mountains.\n\nHighs Saturday and Sunday will be a little warmer than today, \ngenerally in the 50s to low 60s. Behind the cold front Monday,\nhighs will fall back into the 40s and 50s. Then, as ridging builds\nin, highs will return to the 50s to low 60s for Tuesday, before\npotentially trending towards 50s for midweek. Archer",
